{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And the kohen, etc.</b> In his stead, of his sons — in perek ha-techelet (Menachot 51a) they expounded that it speaks of a Kohen Gadol who died and they had not yet appointed another in his stead; his son of the deceased shall bring it in his place, and that is the verse's saying <b>in his stead, of his sons.</b> And they expounded the mem of מִבָּנָיו for another exposition, as it is taught there: ״בָּנָיו״ — these are ordinary kohanim, or is it perhaps only Kohanim Gedolim? Since it says ״וְהַכֹּהֵן הַמָּשִׁיחַ תַּחְתָּיו מִבָּנָיו״ — behold, a Kohen Gadol is stated; how do I establish ״בָּנָיו״? As referring to ordinary kohanim. And the Gemara concludes there that from the verse's saying ״מִבָּנָיו״ it is expounded thus." "And in Horayot, chapter three (Horayot 11a), they said further: A Kohen Gadol who is the son of a Kohen Gadol — does he require anointing? From where do we know it? For it is written ״וְהַכֹּהֵן הַמָּשִׁיחַ תַּחְתָּיו מִבָּנָיו״. Say that the verse reads ״וְהַכֹּהֵן מִתַּחְתָּיו מִבָּנָיו״ — what then is ״הַמָּשִׁיחַ״? etc., end of quotation. And it is difficult: does he not need to say ״הַמָּשִׁיחַ״ to teach about the ״בָּנָיו״ mentioned at the beginning that they are ordinary kohanim, according to the statement of the Tanna in perek ha-techelet? And one may say that to teach about the ״בָּנָיו״ mentioned at the beginning that they are ordinary kohanim is well-understood even had it not said ״הַמָּשִׁיחַ״ — for why do I need two verses for one kohen? Rather, surely ״בָּנָיו״ at the beginning is expounded as referring to ordinary kohanim, and ״בָּנָיו״ at the end refers to Kohanim Gedolim, and it was not necessary to say ״הַמָּשִׁיחַ״ except for the exposition requiring anointing of a Kohen Gadol who is the son of a Kohen Gadol." "Yet it is still difficult in the verse why it said <b>תַּחְתָּיו מִבָּנָיו</b>, when it should have said ״מִבָּנָיו תַּחְתָּיו״. Perhaps it intends to attach בָּנָיו to ״יַעֲשֶׂה אוֹתָהּ״ in order to indicate the exposition of perek ha-techelet (Menachot 27b) that if a Kohen Gadol died and they had not yet appointed another in his place — his son brings it in his place. And this is the measure of the verse: ״וְהַכֹּהֵן הַמָּשִׁיחַ תַּחְתָּיו יַעֲשֶׂה״, and if he has not yet been appointed — from among the sons of the deceased, he shall make it. And if the verse had said ״הַכֹּהֵן הַמָּשִׁיחַ מִבָּנָיו תַּחְתָּיו״, then the intention would be that there is no obligation upon his son to bring it unless he be תַּחְתָּיו, for so it implies specifically תַּחְתָּיו, meaning that he was appointed or that he is fit to stand in his stead. But from its saying ״מִבָּנָיו יַעֲשֶׂה״ it implies that any heir shall bring it. And according to Rabbi Shimon, who disagrees and holds that if they did not appoint another in his stead it is brought from public funds, it said ״תַּחְתָּיו מִבָּנָיו״ to attach מִבָּנָיו to ״יַעֲשֶׂה״, to say that no other person is fit to enter in place of the kohen except his son. After I explained this, I found that they indeed expounded it thus in Torat Kohanim, for one who understands the meaning of the baraitot correctly."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:53:46.133774166Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}